  "findings": {
    "decision": "Reject right-buffer preference. Keep accepted optional runtime at 6ced2eb.",
    "reason": "Native derived DOUBLE output improves 7.556%, but the complete DOUBLE-to-Decimal query regresses 19.147% and its median page faults rise from 126459 to 210412 across 41 executions. No runtime change is retained.",
    "remaining_precision_gap": "The complete DOUBLE/FLOAT query time difference is 19.154% before and 42.027% with the rejected candidate in this experiment. The approximately 19% accepted-runtime cost remains.",
    "shared_path": "Shared DOUBLE addition records +0.149% instructions. Its +1.418% timing interval includes zero and is comparable to same-binary control shifts; this is not a zero-overhead claim.",
    "diagnostic_basis": "Six fixed captures of the accepted binary show sampled heap growth from Int64-to-Float64 casts in DOUBLE queries and Float32-to-Decimal casts in the FLOAT control. Shrink stacks include release of a binary operand and release of a consumed RecordBatch. All 220 sampled brk returns are zero. Do not extrapolate sampled stack proportions or use debugger timings as performance evidence.",
    "change": "Try the uniquely owned right array buffer before the left, preserving left-plus-right order, NULL masks, prefix lengths and safe Arrow allocation ownership. Shared and unavailable buffers fall back.",
    "correctness": "80 native binary tests including 128 ownership/NULL/side combinations, one both-owned NaN/signed-zero case, scalar and length-error cases; 37 planner and 28 runner tests including four Delta lifecycle tests; 258 unchanged benchmark checks/plans, 6784 unchanged SQL observations/classifications, 116 unchanged Delta comparisons.",
    "coverage_limit": "Finite non-NULL performance inputs at batch size 8192. Existing Spark differences remain.",
    "next_step": "Investigate fusing the existing Int64-to-Float64 cast into native addition so its temporary array is not allocated. Preserve the cast rounding and operand evaluation order; prefer removing an allocation over adjusting heap lifetime further."
  },
